    expectedAnswer: "In competitive programming, fast input/output means parsing input once, avoiding unnecessary allocations, and producing output efficiently. In a browser/frontend environment, this mostly translates to batching parsing and serialization rather than writing one output operation per item.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Parse whitespace-separated integers.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction parseInts(input: string): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n  let value = 0;\n  let sign = 1;\n  let inNumber = false;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i <= input.length; i++) {\n    const code = i < input.length ? input.charCodeAt(i) : 32;\n\n    if (code === 45) {\n      sign = -1;\n    } else if (code >= 48 && code <= 57) {\n      value = value * 10 + (code - 48);\n      inNumber = true;\n    } else if (inNumber) {\n      result.push(sign * value);\n      value = 0;\n      sign = 1;\n      inNumber = false;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction parseIntsUsingBuiltIn(input: string): number[] {\n  return input.trim() === \"\"\n    ? []\n    : input.trim().split(/\\s+/).map(Number);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n`\"10 20\\n-5\"` → `[10,20,-5]`\n\nComplexity:\nBoth are O(n) in the input string length.\nThe manual parser minimizes intermediate strings, while `split` is simpler and usually preferable when input size is moderate.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nFirst understand the input format; optimization should be driven by constraints, not habit.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
