    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Implement merge sort to understand a predictable O(n log n) sort.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction mergeSort(values: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  if (values.length <= 1) return [...values];\n\n  const middle = Math.floor(values.length / 2);\n  const left = mergeSort(values.slice(0, middle));\n  const right = mergeSort(values.slice(middle));\n\n  const result: number[] = [];\n  let i = 0;\n  let j = 0;\n\n  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {\n    if (left[i]! <= right[j]!) {\n      result.push(left[i]!);\n      i++;\n    } else {\n      result.push(right[j]!);\n      j++;\n    }\n  }\n\n  while (i < left.length) result.push(left[i++]!);\n  while (j < right.length) result.push(right[j++]!);\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction sortUsingBuiltIn(values: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  return [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[5,2,4,1] → [1,2,4,5]\n\nComplexity:\nMerge sort O(n log n) time and O(n) auxiliary space.\nJavaScript `sort` is implementation-dependent internally, so in interviews state the API usage rather than claiming a specific engine algorithm.\n\nDecision rule:\nSort when ordering enables a later linear or logarithmic pass.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
