    expectedAnswer: "Prefix/suffix techniques precompute information for everything to the left or right of each position. This turns repeated range or neighbor calculations into constant-time lookups after linear preprocessing.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Product of array except self.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction productExceptSelf(nums: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  const result = new Array<number>(nums.length).fill(1);\n\n  let prefix = 1;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    result[i] = prefix;\n    prefix *= nums[i]!;\n  }\n\n  let suffix = 1;\n\n  for (let i = nums.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    result[i]! *= suffix;\n    suffix *= nums[i]!;\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction productExceptSelfUsingReduce(nums: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  return nums.map((_, index) => {\n    return nums\n      .filter((_, current) => current !== index)\n      .reduce((product, value) => product * value, 1);\n  });\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[1,2,3,4] → [24,12,8,6]\n\nComplexity:\nPrefix/suffix approach O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space besides the output.\nThe straightforward filter/reduce approach is O(n²).\n\nInterview takeaway:\nWhenever the result for index i depends on \"everything before\" and/or \"everything after\", think prefix/suffix.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
