    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Sort unique values and map each original coordinate to its rank.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction compressCoordinates(values: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  const sorted = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const unique: number[] = [];\n\n  for (const value of sorted) {\n    if (unique.length === 0 || unique[unique.length - 1] !== value) {\n      unique.push(value);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const rank = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < unique.length; i++) {\n    rank.set(unique[i]!, i);\n  }\n\n  return values.map((value) => rank.get(value)!);\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction compressCoordinatesUsingSet(values: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  const unique = [...new Set(values)].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const rank = new Map(unique.map((value, index) => [value, index]));\n  return values.map((value) => rank.get(value)!);\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[1000000, 20, 1000000, 500]\n→ [2,0,2,1]\n\nComplexity:\nO(n log n) time due to sorting and O(n) space.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nCompression preserves ordering/equality, not the original numeric distances.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
