    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Safe positive modulo helper and fast exponentiation.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction mod(value: number, modulus: number): number {\n  const remainder = value % modulus;\n  return remainder >= 0 ? remainder : remainder + modulus;\n}\n\nfunction modPow(\n  base: number,\n  exponent: number,\n  modulus: number,\n): number {\n  if (modulus <= 0) throw new Error(\"modulus must be positive\");\n\n  let result = 1 % modulus;\n  let current = mod(base, modulus);\n  let power = exponent;\n\n  while (power > 0) {\n    if (power % 2 === 1) {\n      result = (result * current) % modulus;\n    }\n\n    current = (current * current) % modulus;\n    power = Math.floor(power / 2);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-style helper:\n```ts\nfunction modularPowerUsingBigInt(\n  base: bigint,\n  exponent: bigint,\n  modulus: bigint,\n): bigint {\n  let result = 1n % modulus;\n  let current = ((base % modulus) + modulus) % modulus;\n  let power = exponent;\n\n  while (power > 0n) {\n    if (power % 2n === 1n) result = (result * current) % modulus;\n    current = (current * current) % modulus;\n    power /= 2n;\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n2^10 mod 1000 → 24.\n\nComplexity:\nO(log exponent) time and O(1) auxiliary space.\nUse BigInt when exact integer range exceeds safe Number limits.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nReduce after multiplication/addition when the modulus rules permit it, and understand negative remainder behavior in JavaScript.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
