    expectedAnswer: "The Sieve of Eratosthenes finds all prime numbers up to n by initially marking numbers as prime candidates and repeatedly crossing out multiples of each discovered prime.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Only begin crossing multiples from p² because smaller multiples were handled by smaller primes.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction sieve(n: number): number[] {\n  if (n < 2) return [];\n\n  const isPrime = new Array<boolean>(n + 1).fill(true);\n  isPrime[0] = false;\n  isPrime[1] = false;\n\n  for (let p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {\n    if (!isPrime[p]) continue;\n\n    for (let multiple = p * p; multiple <= n; multiple += p) {\n      isPrime[multiple] = false;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const primes: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let value = 2; value <= n; value++) {\n    if (isPrime[value]) primes.push(value);\n  }\n\n  return primes;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN filter alternative:\n```ts\nfunction primesUsingTrialDivision(n: number): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let value = 2; value <= n; value++) {\n    let prime = true;\n\n    for (let divisor = 2; divisor * divisor <= value; divisor++) {\n      if (value % divisor === 0) {\n        prime = false;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (prime) result.push(value);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nn=20 → [2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19]\n\nComplexity:\nSieve O(n log log n) time and O(n) space.\nTrial division is much slower for generating many primes.\n\nInterview takeaway:\nUse a sieve when many primality questions or all primes up to n are needed.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
