    expectedAnswer: "Combinatorics problems count arrangements or selections. A common competitive-programming identity is `nCr = n! / (r!(n-r)!)`, but direct factorials overflow quickly, so iterative formulas or modular arithmetic are preferred.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the competitive-programming requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Compute combinations exactly for values that stay within safe Number range.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction nCr(n: number, r: number): number {\n  if (r < 0 || r > n) return 0;\n\n  const k = Math.min(r, n - r);\n  let result = 1;\n\n  for (let i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\n    result = (result * (n - k + i)) / i;\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / Pascal DP:\n```ts\nfunction nCrUsingDP(n: number, r: number): number {\n  if (r < 0 || r > n) return 0;\n\n  const row = new Array<number>(r + 1).fill(0);\n  row[0] = 1;\n\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n    for (let j = Math.min(i, r); j >= 1; j--) {\n      row[j] = row[j]! + row[j - 1]!;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return row[r]!;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n5 choose 2 = 10.\n\nComplexity:\nMultiplicative version O(min(r,n-r)) time, O(1) auxiliary space.\nPascal DP O(nr) time and O(r) space.\n\nImportant:\nFor competitive-programming moduli and very large n, use factorials/inverse factorials with modular arithmetic when the mathematical conditions permit.\n\nStep 5 — Interview/contest takeaway:\nMatch the algorithm to the constraints and explain the complexity before submitting.",
